window.QUIZ_BANK['api-postman'] = (window.QUIZ_BANK['api-postman']||[]).concat([
{"id":2711,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"What does the acronym REST stand for in web API architecture?","options":["Remote Execution System Transfer","Representational State Transfer","Reliable Entity Storage Protocol","Request Endpoint System Transformation"],"answer":1,"explanation":"REST stands for Representational State Transfer, an architectural style created by Roy Fielding in his 2000 doctoral dissertation. It defines a set of constraints for creating scalable, stateless web services that communicate using standard HTTP verbs and representations."},
{"id":2712,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"Which HTTP method is specifically designed to retrieve data from a server without causing any modifications or side effects?","options":["POST","GET","PUT","PATCH"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The GET method is used strictly to request data from a specified resource on a server. Under HTTP specifications, GET requests must be safe and idempotent, meaning they should never modify server-side state or produce side effects regardless of how many times they are executed."},
{"id":2713,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"Which HTTP method is primarily used to submit data to a server to create a new subordinate resource?","options":["GET","POST","DELETE","OPTIONS"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The POST method sends data to the server to create a new resource under a specified parent URI. Unlike PUT, POST is neither safe nor idempotent, as sending identical POST requests repeatedly will typically result in creating multiple distinct resources with unique server-generated identifiers."},
{"id":2714,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the primary architectural difference between the HTTP PUT and PATCH methods?","options":["PUT deletes resources whereas PATCH updates them","PUT performs complete resource replacement, while PATCH applies partial updates","PATCH is idempotent while PUT is non-idempotent","PUT only works with XML payloads, whereas PATCH only accepts JSON"],"answer":1,"explanation":"HTTP PUT replaces the entire target resource with the uploaded payload, requiring the client to supply the complete representation. In contrast, HTTP PATCH applies a delta or partial set of modifications to the existing resource without requiring the full entity representation to be transmitted."},
{"id":2715,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"Which HTTP method is used by clients to request the deletion of a resource identified by a specific URI?","options":["DELETE","PURGE","REMOVE","DROP"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The DELETE method requests that the origin server remove the resource identified by the target Request-URI. When implemented properly according to RFC specifications, DELETE operations are idempotent because repeated requests to delete the same resource will yield the same resulting state."},
{"id":2716,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the purpose of the HTTP OPTIONS method in RESTful services?","options":["To configure server-side environment variables","To describe the communication options and permitted HTTP methods for the target resource","To upgrade an HTTP connection to a secure TLS WebSocket connection","To validate JSON schemas against database models"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The HTTP OPTIONS method returns the communication options available for the target resource, typically returning allowed methods in the \"Allow\" header. It is extensively used in Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) preflight requests by web browsers before initiating cross-origin cross-domain API calls."},
{"id":2717,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How does the HTTP HEAD method differ from the standard HTTP GET method?","options":["HEAD only returns the HTTP request headers sent by the client","HEAD returns identical headers to GET but completely omits the response body","HEAD encrypts the response body using asymmetrical keys","HEAD requires administrative authentication before processing"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The HTTP HEAD method is identical to GET in terms of headers and status codes returned by the server, but it instructs the server never to transmit a response body. This makes HEAD ideal for checking resource existence, validity, content length, or cache headers without downloading payload data."},
{"id":2718,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does it mean for an HTTP method to be defined as \"idempotent\"?","options":["The method executes in under one second on the server","Making multiple identical requests produces the same side-effect on the server as making a single request","The method does not transmit any request headers or cookies","The response payload is encrypted during transmission"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An HTTP method is idempotent if the side-effects of making multiple identical requests are identical to the side-effects of a single request. GET, HEAD, PUT, and DELETE are idempotent according to RFC 7231 specifications, whereas POST and PATCH are generally non-idempotent."},
{"id":2719,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Which of the following HTTP methods is considered BOTH safe and idempotent?","options":["POST","DELETE","GET","PATCH"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A safe HTTP method is one that does not alter server state (read-only), while an idempotent method produces the same resulting server state regardless of how many times it is called. GET and HEAD are both safe and idempotent. DELETE and PUT are idempotent but not safe."},
{"id":2720,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"In HTTP status code classification, which numeric range represents successful operations?","options":["1xx","2xx","3xx","4xx"],"answer":1,"explanation":"HTTP 2xx status codes indicate that the client request was successfully received, understood, and accepted by the server. Common examples include 200 OK for standard retrievals, 201 Created for new resource generation, and 204 No Content for successful updates or deletions without body return."},
{"id":2721,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"When a POST request successfully creates a new database entity, which HTTP status code and response header should ideally be returned?","options":["200 OK with Content-Encoding header","201 Created with Location header pointing to the new resource URI","202 Accepted with Retry-After header","204 No Content with Set-Cookie header"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The 201 Created status code informs the client that the request has been fulfilled and has resulted in one or more new resources being created. Standard REST design dictates supplying the Location response header containing the absolute or relative URI referencing the newly created resource."},
{"id":2722,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does the HTTP 204 No Content status code signify to the calling client?","options":["The requested resource could not be found in the database","The server successfully processed the request, but is intentionally returning no response body","The server timed out before finding any content to return","The client is not permitted to receive the body payload"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Status code 204 No Content confirms that the server has successfully fulfilled the request and there is no additional content to send in the response payload body. It is frequently returned for successful DELETE operations or PUT/PATCH updates where returning entity bodies is unnecessary."},
{"id":2723,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Which HTTP status code signifies that a request has been accepted for processing, but the processing has not yet been completed?","options":["200 OK","201 Created","202 Accepted","206 Partial Content"],"answer":2,"explanation":"HTTP 202 Accepted indicates that the request has been received and accepted for processing, but processing has not finished or may even be batch-scheduled asynchronously. It prevents client blocking during long-running background tasks such as video encoding, bulk imports, or report generation."},
{"id":2724,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does the HTTP 206 Partial Content status code indicate?","options":["The server is sending only a portion of the resource, typically in response to a Range header from the client","The server encountered an error while streaming half of the body","The database transaction was partially rolled back","The client requested an incomplete JSON payload"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Status code 206 Partial Content is sent in response to an HTTP Range header requested by the client. It is widely utilized for streaming video/audio and resuming interrupted multipart downloads by delivering only requested byte ranges."},
{"id":2725,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"What category of HTTP status codes represents redirection messages (e.g., 301, 302, 304)?","options":["1xx","2xx","3xx","5xx"],"answer":2,"explanation":"HTTP 3xx status codes indicate redirection, requiring the client user agent to take further action to complete the request. For example, 301 indicates permanent resource relocation, 302 indicates temporary relocation, and 304 Not Modified informs caches that cached resources remain fresh."},
{"id":2726,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the key difference between HTTP 301 Moved Permanently and HTTP 302 Found?","options":["301 is for GET requests only, while 302 is for POST requests only","301 indicates the target resource has been assigned a new permanent URI and caches should update, while 302 indicates a temporary redirect","301 requires re-authentication, whereas 302 preserves JWT tokens","301 redirects to HTTPS only, while 302 redirects to HTTP only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"HTTP 301 Moved Permanently informs search engines and caching proxies that the resource has permanently moved to a new URI, prompting clients and SEO crawlers to update their bookmarks and links. HTTP 302 Found indicates temporary relocation, so the original URI should continue to be used in future requests."},
{"id":2727,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does an HTTP 304 Not Modified status code inform an API consumer about?","options":["The resource cannot be modified due to insufficient write permissions","The client cached version is still valid and fresh, so no response body is transmitted","The database transaction was aborted due to optimistic locking conflict","The requested endpoint has been permanently removed from the server"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An HTTP 304 Not Modified response informs the client that the requested resource has not changed since the timestamp or ETag supplied in conditional request headers (such as If-Modified-Since or If-None-Match). The server sends empty payload, saving significant network bandwidth."},
{"id":2728,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"What fundamental issue is represented by the HTTP 400 Bad Request status code?","options":["The server encountered an unhandled database exception","The server cannot process the request due to perceived client error, such as malformed syntax or invalid payload","The client credentials have expired or were rejected","The downstream gateway timed out while waiting for a response"],"answer":1,"explanation":"HTTP 400 Bad Request indicates that the server cannot or will not process the request due to something perceived to be a client error (e.g., malformed request syntax, invalid query parameters, invalid request routing framing, or deceptive request routing)."},
{"id":2729,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the precise distinction between HTTP 401 Unauthorized and HTTP 403 Forbidden status codes?","options":["401 indicates server failure, while 403 indicates client syntax failure","401 means authentication is required or failed; 403 means the authenticated user lacks authorization/permission to access the resource","401 is used for temporary failures, while 403 is for permanent blacklisting","401 applies to GET requests, while 403 applies strictly to POST and DELETE requests"],"answer":1,"explanation":"HTTP 401 Unauthorized specifically means unauthenticated—the client must provide valid authentication credentials (or token) to proceed. HTTP 403 Forbidden means the server understands who the caller is, but that authenticated identity possesses insufficient role permissions to access the resource."},
{"id":2730,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"Which HTTP status code is returned when an API endpoint or target resource does not exist on the server?","options":["400 Bad Request","404 Not Found","405 Method Not Allowed","410 Gone"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The 404 Not Found status code indicates that the origin server did not find a current representation for the target resource or is unwilling to disclose that one exists. It is the standard REST response for non-existent routes or resource IDs."},
{"id":2731,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"When an endpoint only supports GET and POST, but a client sends a DELETE request, which status code should the API return?","options":["400 Bad Request","404 Not Found","405 Method Not Allowed","501 Not Implemented"],"answer":2,"explanation":"HTTP 405 Method Not Allowed indicates that the method received in the request-line is known by the origin server but not supported by the target resource. The server MUST generate an \"Allow\" header in a 405 response containing a list of valid methods for the target."},
{"id":2732,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the difference between HTTP 404 Not Found and HTTP 410 Gone?","options":["404 means the resource is permanently deleted, while 410 means it is temporarily unavailable","404 indicates the resource is not found currently without indicating permanence, while 410 explicitly confirms the resource was intentionally deleted and will not return","404 is a server error, while 410 is a client error","404 is for HTML pages, while 410 is for JSON endpoints"],"answer":1,"explanation":"HTTP 410 Gone indicates that access to the target resource is no longer available at the origin server and that this condition is likely to be permanent. Unlike 404, 410 encourages clients and search engines to purge the reference permanently."},
{"id":2733,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Which HTTP status code is most appropriate when a resource update cannot be completed due to a state conflict, such as concurrent edits?","options":["409 Conflict","412 Precondition Failed","422 Unprocessable Entity","400 Bad Request"],"answer":0,"explanation":"HTTP 409 Conflict indicates that the request could not be processed because of conflict in the current state of the resource, such as an edit conflict in collaborative systems, duplicate key violations, or attempting to delete a resource referenced by active foreign keys."},
{"id":2734,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does the HTTP 415 Unsupported Media Type status code indicate?","options":["The server does not support the HTTP protocol version","The server refuses to accept the request because the payload format (Content-Type) is in an unsupported format","The client refused the server response compression","The requested video media codec is outdated"],"answer":1,"explanation":"HTTP 415 Unsupported Media Type indicates that the origin server refuses to service the request because the payload is in a format not supported by this method on the target resource (for example, sending XML when the server only accepts application/json)."},
{"id":2735,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does the HTTP 422 Unprocessable Entity status code signify in modern RESTful APIs?","options":["The request body format is not supported by the media handler","The server understands the content type and syntax of the request, but was unable to process the contained instructions due to semantic validation errors","The endpoint is temporarily unavailable due to routine server maintenance","The request rate has exceeded the quota configured for the API key"],"answer":1,"explanation":"HTTP 422 Unprocessable Entity means the server understands the content type of the request entity and the syntax is correct, but it was unable to process the contained instructions due to semantic errors, such as missing required domain fields or failing business rules."},
{"id":2736,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Which HTTP status code is explicitly designed to indicate that the client has sent too many requests in a given amount of time (rate limiting)?","options":["408 Request Timeout","429 Too Many Requests","503 Service Unavailable","504 Gateway Timeout"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Status code 429 Too Many Requests indicates that the user has sent too many requests in a given amount of time (\"rate limiting\"). The response representations SHOULD include details explaining the condition, and MAY include a Retry-After header indicating how long to wait before making a new request."},
{"id":2737,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"What does an HTTP 500 Internal Server Error status code indicate?","options":["The client supplied an invalid OAuth token format","The server encountered an unexpected condition that prevented it from fulfilling the request","The client DNS resolver failed to resolve host IP address","The client network connection dropped during payload transmission"],"answer":1,"explanation":"HTTP 500 Internal Server Error is a generic error response indicating that the server encountered an unexpected internal condition, runtime exception, or unhandled crash that prevented it from fulfilling the incoming request. It always points to a server-side problem rather than a client error."},
{"id":2738,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the key difference between HTTP 502 Bad Gateway and HTTP 504 Gateway Timeout status codes?","options":["502 is a client-side network error, while 504 is a server-side error","502 means an intermediate proxy/gateway received an invalid response from upstream, while 504 means the gateway timed out waiting for upstream response","502 indicates lack of authentication, while 504 indicates rate limit expiration","502 is deprecated in HTTP/2, whereas 504 is mandatory in HTTP/3"],"answer":1,"explanation":"HTTP 502 Bad Gateway means a server acting as a gateway or reverse proxy received an invalid response from the inbound upstream server. HTTP 504 Gateway Timeout means that the gateway or reverse proxy did not receive a timely response from the upstream server within its configured timeout window."},
{"id":2739,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does the HTTP 503 Service Unavailable status code signify?","options":["The requested endpoint URI does not exist on the server","The server is currently unable to handle the request due to temporary overloading or scheduled maintenance","The server does not support the HTTP protocol version used in the request","The server refused the connection because the client IP is blacklisted"],"answer":1,"explanation":"HTTP 503 Service Unavailable indicates that the server is currently unable to handle the request due to a temporary overloading or maintenance of the server. This is generally a temporary state, often accompanied by a Retry-After header advising when the client may retry."},
{"id":2740,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"Which HTTP header is used by a client to inform the server about the data format of the request body being sent?","options":["Accept","Content-Type","User-Agent","Authorization"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Content-Type header indicates the media type (MIME type) of the entity-body sent to the receiver (e.g., application/json, application/xml, or multipart/form-data). It tells the server how to parse the incoming request body payload."},
{"id":2741,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"Which HTTP header is used by a client to declare the media types that it is willing to receive back from the server in the response?","options":["Content-Type","Accept","Host","Origin"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Accept request HTTP header advertises which content types, expressed as MIME types, the client is able to understand and expects back from the server (e.g., Accept: application/json). The server uses content negotiation to select one of the proposals."},
{"id":2742,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Which HTTP header carries credentials for authenticating a client user agent with a server using Bearer tokens?","options":["Proxy-Authorization: Token <token>","Authorization: Bearer <token>","Authentication: Key <token>","X-Auth-Token: Bearer <token>"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The standard HTTP header for carrying authentication credentials is the \"Authorization\" header. In token-based schemes such as JWT or OAuth 2.0, it takes the format \"Authorization: Bearer <token_string>\", where \"Bearer\" defines the authentication scheme type."},
{"id":2743,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"In HTTP caching mechanisms, what is the role of an ETag (Entity Tag) header?","options":["It tracks the geographic IP location of the originating client","It provides an opaque identifier assigned by a web server to a specific version of a resource for cache validation","It stores the session state of the database connection pool","It encrypts the payload headers against MITM inspection"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An ETag (Entity Tag) is an HTTP response header that acts as an opaque validator assigned by a web server to a specific version of a resource. Clients can send the ETag back in an \"If-None-Match\" header to determine if cached contents are unchanged, saving bandwidth via 304 responses."},
{"id":2744,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the primary purpose of the \"Idempotency-Key\" custom HTTP header in financial and payment processing APIs?","options":["To generate AES encryption keys dynamically per request","To prevent duplicate transactions when network retries occur on non-idempotent POST requests","To authenticate user roles against LDAP directory groups","To compress large financial ledger files using GZIP"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Idempotency-Key header allows API clients to safely retry POST requests (such as credit card charges) without accidentally executing duplicate operations. The server records the unique key and returns the identical cached response if a request with the same key is retransmitted."},
{"id":2745,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"Which of the following is one of the six core architectural constraints of REST defined by Roy Fielding?","options":["Mandatory GraphQL schema definition","Statelessness","Strict binary protocol serialization","Two-phase commit distributed transactions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fielding's six guiding constraints of REST are: Client-Server Architecture, Statelessness, Cacheability, Layered System, Code-on-Demand (optional), and Uniform Interface. Statelessness requires that each request from client to server must contain all necessary context without relying on server-stored sessions."},
{"id":2746,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does the \"Statelessness\" constraint in REST architecture mandate?","options":["The server must never store any persistent records in a database","No client session state is stored on the server between requests; every request must contain all necessary context to be understood","Client applications must not maintain local browser storage","API servers must run in serverless container environments only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Statelessness in REST requires that no client session context is stored on the server between requests. Every request from client to server must contain all the information necessary to understand and complete the request (such as authentication tokens and parameters), improving server scalability and reliability."},
{"id":2747,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"advanced","question":"What does HATEOAS stand for in REST architectural maturity (Richardson Maturity Model Level 3)?","options":["Hierarchical Application Transmission Enabling Online Authentication Security","Hypermedia As The Engine Of Application State","Hypertext Authoring Tools Enabling Open Application Services","High Availability Transfer Engine Over Automated Sockets"],"answer":1,"explanation":"HATEOAS stands for Hypermedia As The Engine Of Application State. Under this Level 3 maturity model constraint, API responses contain dynamic hypermedia links (URLs) that guide the client on available actions, state transitions, and related resources without requiring hardcoded client endpoints."},
{"id":2748,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Which of the following represents the most RESTful URI naming convention for fetching comments belonging to a specific post?","options":["GET /get_post_comments?post_id=45","GET /posts/45/comments","POST /viewCommentsForPost45","GET /comments_service/retrieveByPostId/45"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In RESTful design, URIs should represent resources using nouns rather than verbs, organized in hierarchical relationships. \"GET /posts/45/comments\" clearly communicates that the client is retrieving the sub-collection of comments associated with the parent post identified by ID 45."},
{"id":2749,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"What is the primary transport layer security difference between HTTP and HTTPS?","options":["HTTPS uses UDP instead of TCP for faster payload streaming","HTTPS encrypts all communication traffic using TLS/SSL over TCP port 443","HTTPS compresses request payloads using Brotli algorithms","HTTPS disallows the use of JSON payloads in REST endpoints"],"answer":1,"explanation":"HTTPS (Hypertext Transfer Protocol Secure) encrypts all communication between the client and server using TLS (Transport Layer Security) or SSL, typically operating on TCP port 443. This prevents eavesdropping, tampering, and Man-in-the-Middle (MITM) attacks on sensitive API payloads."},
{"id":2750,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What happens during the TLS handshake when establishing a secure HTTPS connection between an API client and server?","options":["The database schema is verified against client models","The client and server authenticate each other via certificates and negotiate cryptographic session keys","The server converts all XML payloads to JSON format","The client downloads the full OpenAPI specification file"],"answer":1,"explanation":"During the TLS handshake, the client and server exchange hello messages to agree on cipher suites, the server presents its digital SSL/TLS certificate for identity validation, and they securely exchange/derive symmetric session keys used to encrypt subsequent HTTP traffic."},
{"id":2751,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"Which data format is native to JavaScript, lightweight, text-based, and the predominant standard for modern RESTful API payloads?","options":["XML","JSON","YAML","Protocol Buffers"],"answer":1,"explanation":"JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a lightweight, human-readable data interchange format based on a subset of the JavaScript programming language. It is easily parsed natively in almost all programming languages and is the dominant serialization format for REST APIs."},
{"id":2752,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Which of the following is a key structural feature supported by XML that is NOT natively supported in standard JSON format?","options":["Nested objects and arrays","Key-value mapping","Attributes on elements, comments, and formal XSD schema validation","Boolean data types"],"answer":2,"explanation":"XML supports element attributes (e.g., <item id=\"1\">), inline comments (<!-- comment -->), and formal structural validation using XML Schema Definition (XSD) and DTDs. Standard JSON does not natively support element attributes or inline comments."},
{"id":2753,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Which API versioning strategy embeds the version identifier directly within the URI path (e.g., /api/v1/users)?","options":["Header Versioning","URI Path Versioning","Content Negotiation Versioning","Query Parameter Versioning"],"answer":1,"explanation":"URI Path Versioning embeds the version indicator directly in the route URL (e.g., https://api.example.com/v1/orders). It is widely favored for its explicitness, ease of testing in browsers and API tools, and compatibility with standard caching proxies."},
{"id":2754,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How does API versioning via Content Negotiation (Accept header) work?","options":["The client passes the version number inside the URL query string (?version=1)","The client specifies the requested version in the Accept header (e.g., Accept: application/vnd.company.v1+json)","The server modifies the response body structure based on client IP","The client includes the version in the User-Agent header"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Content Negotiation versioning uses custom MIME types in the \"Accept\" header (e.g., Accept: application/vnd.myapi.v2+json). This keeps URIs clean and permanent while allowing the client to request specific representation versions. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2755,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"In query parameter versioning, how does an API client indicate that it requires version 2 of the users endpoint?","options":["GET /users?version=2 or GET /users?v=2","POST /users/header/v2","GET /users#version2","OPTIONS /users/version(2)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Query parameter versioning passes the version as a key-value query parameter in the URL, such as \"GET /users?v=2\" or \"GET /users?api-version=2.0\". If omitted, the server typically routes the request to a default fallback version."},
{"id":2756,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the primary advantage of Custom Header Versioning (e.g., X-API-Version: 2) over URI Path Versioning?","options":["It keeps resource URIs clean, canonical, and uniform across different schema iterations","It enables caching on legacy proxy servers without any header inspection","It eliminates the need for authentication tokens","It guarantees zero downtime deployments on relational databases"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Custom Header Versioning keeps resource URIs identical across iterations (e.g., /users remains /users), adhering to the architectural principle that URIs identify resources rather than representation versions. Clients pass headers such as \"X-API-Version: 2\" to request specific schemas."},
{"id":2757,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"What is Basic Authentication in HTTP APIs?","options":["A scheme where username and password are joined with a colon and encoded in Base64 within the Authorization header","An OAuth 2.0 flow using cryptographic PKCE verification","A hardware token MFA protocol","A cookie-based session handshake using TLS certificates"],"answer":0,"explanation":"HTTP Basic Authentication transmits credentials as a single string formatted as \"username:password\" encoded in Base64, passed in the \"Authorization: Basic <base64_string>\" header. Because Base64 is simple encoding (not encryption), it must strictly be used over HTTPS."},
{"id":2758,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What are the three distinct components separated by dots that comprise a JSON Web Token (JWT)?","options":["Protocol, Domain, and Endpoint","Header, Payload, and Signature","Client ID, Client Secret, and Scope","Issuer, Audience, and Timestamp"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A JWT consists of three parts separated by dots (header.payload.signature). The Header defines the token type and signing algorithm; the Payload contains claims (user ID, expiration, roles); and the Signature is generated by hashing the header and payload with a secret or private key."},
{"id":2759,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Why is an API Key typically passed in a custom header (e.g., X-API-Key) rather than in a URL query parameter?","options":["Query parameters are forbidden by the HTTP/1.1 specification","URLs with query parameters are frequently stored in plain text in browser history, proxy logs, and web server access logs","Custom headers compress the payload size by 50%","API keys in headers bypass firewall inspection"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Passing sensitive API keys in query parameters exposes them to leakage because full URLs are frequently logged in plain text in proxy servers, browser history, server access logs, and referrer headers. Passing keys in HTTP headers prevents accidental exposure in URL logs."},
{"id":2760,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"advanced","question":"What is Mutual TLS (mTLS) in API communication?","options":["A mechanism where both the client and the server present and verify digital certificates to establish mutual cryptographic identity","A method where two different passwords are typed simultaneously","A dual-tunnel VPN connection over SSH","An OAuth protocol where tokens are signed by two distinct authorization servers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Mutual TLS (mTLS) is a process where both the client and the server authenticate each other simultaneously during the TLS handshake by presenting and verifying each other's X.509 digital certificates, ensuring zero-trust security for microservices and B2B API integrations."},
{"id":2761,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"In the Postman desktop application, what is a \"Collection\"?","options":["A database connection string","A group of saved HTTP/API requests that can be organized into folders and run together","A compiled binary file that runs on the operating system","A list of registered user passwords"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A Postman Collection is a group of saved requests that can be organized into hierarchical folders. Collections allow API developers and testers to group related endpoints, share API suites with teams, configure collection-level authorization, and execute automated test runs."},
{"id":2762,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"What syntax is used in Postman to reference a variable inside the URL, headers, or request body builder?","options":["${variable_name}","{{variable_name}}","$(variable_name)","[[variable_name]]"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Postman uses double curly braces {{variable_name}} to reference variables in URLs, parameters, headers, and raw request body payloads. When the request executes, Postman automatically resolves and interpolates the variable according to current scope hierarchy."},
{"id":2763,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the correct variable scope resolution hierarchy in Postman, ordered from broadest (lowest priority) to narrowest (highest priority)?","options":["Global -> Collection -> Environment -> Data -> Local","Local -> Data -> Environment -> Collection -> Global","Global -> Environment -> Collection -> Data -> Local","Data -> Global -> Environment -> Collection -> Local"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Postman resolves variables from broadest to narrowest: Global -> Collection -> Environment -> Data -> Local. If a variable with the same name exists in multiple scopes, Postman uses the value from the narrowest (highest priority) scope, with Local and Data overriding Environment, Collection, and Global."},
{"id":2764,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"What is a Postman \"Environment\"?","options":["A virtual machine running a Linux operating system","A set of key-value pair variables that allow you to switch contexts (e.g., Development, Staging, Production) effortlessly","The physical office location where Postman runs","A Docker container hosting mock databases"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A Postman Environment is a set of key-value pairs that lets you customize requests for different deployment targets (e.g., Local, QA, Staging, Production). By switching the active environment dropdown, {{baseUrl}} and other credentials automatically map to the selected server."},
{"id":2765,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the difference between \"Initial Value\" and \"Current Value\" for variables in Postman workspace environments?","options":["Initial Value is used only in scripts; Current Value is used only in URLs","Initial Value is synced to Postman cloud servers and shared with team members; Current Value is strictly local to your machine session and never synced","Initial Value is read-only; Current Value can never be overwritten","Initial Value is for strings; Current Value is for numeric types"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In Postman, \"Initial Value\" is synced to the Postman cloud workspace and shared across team collaborators. \"Current Value\" is stored locally on your specific client session and is never synced to cloud servers, making it the secure location for personal API keys, passwords, and tokens."},
{"id":2766,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"Which built-in dynamic variable in Postman generates a random UUID (Universally Unique Identifier)?","options":["{{$guid}}","{{$randomUUID}}","{{$uuid}}","Both {{$guid}} and {{$randomUUID}}"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Postman includes built-in dynamic variables powered by Faker.js. Both {{$guid}} and {{$randomUUID}} generate random v4 UUID strings at runtime. Other dynamic variables include {{$randomEmail}}, {{$randomFirstName}}, {{$timestamp}}, and {{$randomInt}}. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2767,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Which dynamic variable in Postman returns the current Unix timestamp in seconds?","options":["{{$now}}","{{$timestamp}}","{{$currentTime}}","{{$epoch}}"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The dynamic variable {{$timestamp}} in Postman outputs the current Unix timestamp in seconds (e.g., 1718000000). To obtain millisecond timestamps or custom formatted date strings in scripts, testers use Date.now() or moment() within JavaScript code."},
{"id":2768,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"In Postman, what body type option should you select when sending form submissions that include binary file uploads?","options":["raw (application/json)","form-data","x-www-form-urlencoded","binary (raw stream)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The \"form-data\" body option in Postman corresponds to the multipart/form-data content type. It allows individual fields to be configured as either \"Text\" or \"File\", enabling simultaneous transmission of key-value metadata and binary file attachments."},
{"id":2769,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the purpose of the \"x-www-form-urlencoded\" request body format in Postman?","options":["It sends unformatted binary streams directly to sockets","It encodes key-value pairs into a URL-encoded query string format sent inside the HTTP request body","It automatically compiles TypeScript files before sending","It compresses the request body using Base64 encryption"],"answer":1,"explanation":"\"x-www-form-urlencoded\" formats the payload as URL-encoded key-value pairs (key1=value1&key2=value2) in the request body, identical to standard HTML form submissions. Unlike form-data, it does not support file attachments. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2770,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"Where can you inspect the exact raw HTTP request headers and response bytes sent over the wire by Postman?","options":["Postman Collection Settings","Postman Console (bottom toolbar or shortcut Ctrl/Cmd+Alt+C)","Postman Account Profile","Postman Trash tab"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Postman Console (accessible via the bottom-left status bar or shortcut Ctrl+Alt+C / Cmd+Option+C) logs every network call made by Postman, showing exact raw request headers, request payload, response headers, response payload, network timing, and console.log outputs."},
{"id":2771,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"In Postman, what is the execution order of scripts for a single request inside a collection folder?","options":["Request Pre-request -> Folder Pre-request -> Send -> Request Test","Collection Pre-request -> Folder Pre-request -> Request Pre-request -> Request Executes -> Collection Tests -> Folder Tests -> Request Tests","Request Tests -> Folder Tests -> Collection Tests -> Pre-request scripts","Collection Pre-request -> Request Test -> Request Pre-request -> Server Response"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Postman executes scripts hierarchically: 1. Collection Pre-request script, 2. Folder Pre-request script, 3. Request Pre-request script, 4. HTTP Request Execution, 5. Collection Test script, 6. Folder Test script, 7. Request Test script."},
{"id":2772,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How can you programmatically set an Environment variable from inside a Postman script?","options":["pm.environment.set(\"key\", \"value\");","postman.setEnvVar(\"key\", \"value\");","pm.variables.createEnv(\"key\", \"value\");","pm.globals.setEnvironment(\"key\", \"value\");"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The modern Postman Sandbox API uses `pm.environment.set(\"key\", \"value\")` to assign or update variables in the active environment scope. To read the variable back in a script, you invoke `pm.environment.get(\"key\")`. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2773,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you programmatically retrieve a variable in Postman scripts searching across all scopes in hierarchical order?","options":["pm.variables.get(\"varName\")","pm.scope.find(\"varName\")","pm.environment.resolve(\"varName\")","pm.hierarchy.get(\"varName\")"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The method `pm.variables.get(\"varName\")` inspects variables across all available scopes (Local -> Data -> Environment -> Collection -> Global) and returns the value from the highest priority scope currently defining that variable name."},
{"id":2774,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Which method is used to remove or clear an environment variable in a Postman script?","options":["pm.environment.delete(\"key\");","pm.environment.unset(\"key\");","pm.environment.remove(\"key\");","pm.environment.clearKey(\"key\");"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In the Postman script API, `pm.environment.unset(\"key\")` removes a specific key-value pair from the active environment. To clear all variables in the active environment at once, you use `pm.environment.clear()`. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2775,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How can you set a Collection-level variable programmatically inside a Postman script?","options":["pm.collectionVariables.set(\"token\", tokenValue);","pm.collection.set(\"token\", tokenValue);","pm.parent.set(\"token\", tokenValue);","pm.folder.variable(\"token\", tokenValue);"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Postman API provides `pm.collectionVariables.set(key, value)` and `pm.collectionVariables.get(key)` to manage variables scoped exclusively to the parent collection, ensuring values persist with the collection without altering environment profiles. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2776,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"What is the primary purpose of the \"Pre-request Script\" tab in Postman?","options":["To generate final HTML reports","To execute JavaScript code before the HTTP request is sent to the server","To mock server endpoints in cloud containers","To validate JSON schemas after receiving responses"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pre-request scripts run before the HTTP request is transmitted over the wire. They are commonly used for computing dynamic timestamps, generating HMAC cryptographic signatures, calculating checksums, or fetching dynamic pre-requisite authentication tokens."},
{"id":2777,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"What JavaScript library is embedded natively inside Postman's sandbox for assertion testing?","options":["Jasmine and Chai","JUnit and AssertJ","PyTest and unittest","NUnit and Moq"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Postman embeds Chai.js (providing BDD style assertions like expect) and Jasmine syntax inside its JavaScript sandbox runtime. `pm.expect()` is built on top of Chai's BDD assertion library. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2778,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"What is the standard syntax to define a test block in Postman Scripts?","options":["pm.test(\"Test description\", function () { ... });","test.run(\"Test description\", () => { ... });","pm.assert(\"Test description\", function () { ... });","check(\"Test description\", function () { ... });"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In Postman, `pm.test(testName, function)` is the standard wrapper used to define a named test assertion block. If any assertion inside the callback function throws an exception, Postman records that specific test as failed in the Test Results tab."},
{"id":2779,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"Which assertion correctly checks that the HTTP response status code is 200 in Postman?","options":["pm.response.to.have.status(200);","pm.response.isStatus(200);","pm.status.equals(200);","pm.response.code.shouldEqual(200);"],"answer":0,"explanation":"`pm.response.to.have.status(200)` is the idiomatic Postman Chai assertion to verify that the HTTP response code returned by the server equals 200 OK. Alternatively, `pm.expect(pm.response.code).to.eql(200)` can be used. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2780,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you parse a JSON response body into a JavaScript object in a Postman test script?","options":["const data = pm.response.json();","const data = pm.body.parseJSON();","const data = JSON.fromResponse(pm.response);","const data = pm.response.toObject();"],"answer":0,"explanation":"`pm.response.json()` parses the raw JSON text from the response payload into a traversable JavaScript object. If the response is not valid JSON, it throws a parsing error that can be caught or flagged in tests."},
{"id":2781,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Which assertion verifies that the response time is below 500 milliseconds in Postman?","options":["pm.expect(pm.response.responseTime).to.be.below(500);","pm.response.time.isLessThan(500);","pm.expect(pm.response.elapsed).to.be.smallerThan(500);","pm.test.timing(500);"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The expression `pm.expect(pm.response.responseTime).to.be.below(500)` validates that the total server response time in milliseconds is strictly under the 500ms threshold, helping ensure compliance with strict performance service level agreements (SLAs). This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2782,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Which Postman assertion verifies that a specific response header \"Content-Type\" contains \"application/json\"?","options":["pm.expect(pm.response.headers.get(\"Content-Type\")).to.include(\"application/json\");","pm.response.header(\"Content-Type\").has(\"application/json\");","pm.expect(pm.headers.type).to.equal(\"application/json\");","pm.response.assertHeader(\"Content-Type\", \"application/json\");"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Calling `pm.response.headers.get(\"Content-Type\")` extracts the raw string representation of the response header, and chaining `.to.include(\"application/json\")` asserts that the required MIME type is present even when accompanied by charset parameters like UTF-8."},
{"id":2783,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you assert that a JSON response contains a property named \"email\" with a specific value \"user@example.com\"?","options":["pm.expect(pm.response.json().email).to.eql(\"user@example.com\");","pm.response.json().hasKey(\"email\", \"user@example.com\");","pm.expect(pm.response.json()).to.containProperty(\"email\", \"user@example.com\");","pm.json.assert(\"email\", \"user@example.com\");"],"answer":0,"explanation":"By assigning `const jsonData = pm.response.json();`, testers can run `pm.expect(jsonData.email).to.eql(\"user@example.com\")`, which evaluates deep equality between the extracted JSON property value and the expected target string. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2784,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Which assertion verifies that an array named \"items\" in a JSON response is not empty?","options":["pm.expect(jsonData.items).to.be.an(\"array\").that.is.not.empty;","pm.expect(jsonData.items.count).to.be.greaterThan(0);","pm.items.shouldNotBeEmpty();","pm.expect(jsonData.items).to.have.lengthOf(true);"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Chai BDD syntax allows chaining language words: `pm.expect(jsonData.items).to.be.an(\"array\").that.is.not.empty;` validates both that the property is of type array and that its length is strictly greater than 0. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2785,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you validate a response payload against a JSON Schema using Ajv inside a Postman test script?","options":["const Ajv = require(\"ajv\"); const ajv = new Ajv(); pm.expect(ajv.validate(schema, pm.response.json())).to.be.true;","pm.response.validateSchema(schema);","pm.expect(pm.response.json()).to.matchSchema(schema);","pm.schema.validate(schema, pm.response.json());"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Postman's sandbox includes the Ajv (Another JSON Schema Validator) library via `const Ajv = require(\"ajv\");`. You instantiate Ajv, compile/validate the schema with `ajv.validate(schema, pm.response.json())`, and assert the result is true using Chai."},
{"id":2786,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Which Postman assertion verifies that the HTTP response status text is \"Created\"?","options":["pm.response.to.have.status(\"Created\");","pm.response.statusText.shouldBe(\"Created\");","pm.expect(pm.response.status).to.equal(\"Created\");","pm.response.isStatusText(\"Created\");"],"answer":0,"explanation":"`pm.response.to.have.status(\"Created\")` tests the status string reason phrase. Postman's built-in assertion accepts either the integer code `pm.response.to.have.status(201)` or the standard HTTP status string name `pm.response.to.have.status(\"Created\")` seamlessly. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2787,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How can you log messages and variable values to the Postman Console for debugging purposes?","options":["console.log(\"Debug value:\", myVar);","pm.logger.info(\"Debug value:\", myVar);","print(\"Debug value:\", myVar);","System.out.println(\"Debug value:\", myVar);"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Postman scripts execute in a standard JavaScript runtime environment, supporting native `console.log()`, `console.info()`, `console.warn()`, and `console.error()`. Logged messages appear directly in the Postman Console window with full interactive inspection capabilities."},
{"id":2788,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"advanced","question":"How can you send an asynchronous HTTP request from within a Postman Pre-request Script or Test Script?","options":["pm.sendRequest(\"https://api.example.com/token\", function (err, res) { ... });","fetch(\"https://api.example.com/token\").then(...)","pm.http.get(\"https://api.example.com/token\");","pm.ajax({ url: \"https://api.example.com/token\" });"],"answer":0,"explanation":"`pm.sendRequest()` allows you to execute an asynchronous HTTP/HTTPS request inside pre-request or test scripts. It accepts a URL string or full request configuration object and executes a callback function with `(err, response)` upon completion."},
{"id":2789,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What method in Postman Test scripts extracts a property from the response and stores it in an environment variable for subsequent chained requests?","options":["const res = pm.response.json(); pm.environment.set(\"userId\", res.id);","pm.chain.store(\"userId\", pm.response.json().id);","pm.response.extractToEnv(\"id\", \"userId\");","postman.storeVariable(\"userId\", pm.response.body.id);"],"answer":0,"explanation":"To chain API requests, you parse the current response body with `pm.response.json()`, extract the target field (e.g., `res.id`), and call `pm.environment.set(\"userId\", res.id)`. Subsequent requests can reference this value using `{{userId}}`."},
{"id":2790,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Which assertion verifies that a response string contains an expected substring?","options":["pm.expect(pm.response.text()).to.include(\"success\");","pm.response.hasSubstring(\"success\");","pm.expect(pm.response.body).to.matchExact(\"success\");","pm.text.contains(\"success\");"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Calling `pm.response.text()` returns the entire raw response body as a string, and chaining `.to.include(\"success\")` checks whether the target string \"success\" is present anywhere within that body text without requiring full structural parsing."},
{"id":2791,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"advanced","question":"Which external JavaScript library bundled in Postman can be required to manipulate and format dates?","options":["moment","dayjs","date-fns","chrono"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Postman includes the Moment.js library natively in its sandbox. You can access it by calling `const moment = require(\"moment\");`, allowing you to format dates (e.g., `moment().add(1, \"day\").format(\"YYYY-MM-DD\")`) in pre-request and test scripts."},
{"id":2792,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"advanced","question":"Which bundled library in Postman is used to generate cryptographic hashes such as SHA256 or MD5 in Pre-request scripts?","options":["crypto-js","bcrypt","forge","webcrypto"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Postman pre-packages the `crypto-js` library. Developers can use `const CryptoJS = require(\"crypto-js\");` to generate MD5, SHA-1, SHA-256, HMAC, and AES encrypted signatures for API authentication headers. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2793,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you check if a response header \"Cache-Control\" exists in the response in Postman?","options":["pm.expect(pm.response.headers.has(\"Cache-Control\")).to.be.true;","pm.response.hasHeader(\"Cache-Control\");","pm.expect(pm.headers.contains(\"Cache-Control\")).to.be.ok;","pm.response.headers.exists(\"Cache-Control\");"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The method `pm.response.headers.has(\"Cache-Control\")` returns a boolean indicating whether the given header key is present in the server response headers collection, and asserting `.to.be.true` confirms its presence. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2794,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Which assertion verifies that an object has all required properties [\"id\", \"name\", \"role\"]?","options":["pm.expect(jsonData).to.have.all.keys(\"id\", \"name\", \"role\");","pm.expect(jsonData).to.includeAllProperties([\"id\", \"name\", \"role\"]);","pm.expect(jsonData.keys()).to.match([\"id\", \"name\", \"role\"]);","pm.response.hasProperties(\"id\", \"name\", \"role\");"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In Chai BDD assertion syntax within Postman scripts, `pm.expect(jsonData).to.have.all.keys(\"id\", \"name\", \"role\")` asserts that the object contains exactly and only the specified keys without any extra unexpected properties. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2795,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Which assertion verifies that an object contains at least the keys \"id\" and \"name\", even if other keys are present?","options":["pm.expect(jsonData).to.include.all.keys(\"id\", \"name\");","pm.expect(jsonData).to.have.partialKeys(\"id\", \"name\");","pm.expect(jsonData).to.containKeys(\"id\", \"name\");","pm.assert.hasSomeKeys(jsonData, [\"id\", \"name\"]);"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Using `pm.expect(jsonData).to.include.all.keys(\"id\", \"name\")` verifies that the target object contains the specified subset of keys, while permitting extra unlisted properties to exist without failing the test. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2796,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Which Chai assertion checks that a field `user.age` is a number greater than or equal to 18?","options":["pm.expect(jsonData.user.age).to.be.a(\"number\").and.to.be.at.least(18);","pm.expect(jsonData.user.age).isType(\"number\").isGte(18);","pm.expect(jsonData.user.age).to.equalNumber().greaterOrEqual(18);","pm.assert.gte(jsonData.user.age, 18);"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Chai allows chaining assertions: `pm.expect(jsonData.user.age).to.be.a(\"number\").and.to.be.at.least(18)` verifies both data type correctness (numeric) and boundary conditions (minimum value of 18) in a single readable line. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2797,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Which assertion correctly checks that the response cookie \"session_id\" is present?","options":["pm.expect(pm.cookies.has(\"session_id\")).to.be.true;","pm.cookies.check(\"session_id\");","pm.response.hasCookie(\"session_id\");","pm.expect(pm.response.cookie).to.include(\"session_id\");"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The `pm.cookies` object provides inspection of cookies returned by the server. Calling `pm.cookies.has(\"session_id\")` returns a boolean, and asserting `.to.be.true` validates its presence across the active response cookies. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2798,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How can you read the value of a specific cookie named \"auth_token\" in a Postman test script?","options":["const token = pm.cookies.get(\"auth_token\");","const token = pm.response.cookieValue(\"auth_token\");","const token = pm.headers.cookie(\"auth_token\");","const token = pm.request.getCookie(\"auth_token\");"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The method `pm.cookies.get(\"auth_token\")` retrieves the string value of the specified cookie from the response. If the cookie is not present or was not set by the server, it returns `undefined`."},
{"id":2799,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Which assertion checks that a string matches a regular expression (e.g., standard email format) in Postman?","options":["pm.expect(jsonData.email).to.match(/^[^s@]+@[^s@]+.[^s@]+$/);","pm.expect(jsonData.email).to.regex(/^[^s@]+@[^s@]+.[^s@]+$/);","pm.expect(jsonData.email).pattern(/^[^s@]+@[^s@]+.[^s@]+$/);","pm.response.matchRegex(\"email\", /^[^s@]+@[^s@]+.[^s@]+$/);"],"answer":0,"explanation":"`pm.expect(string).to.match(regex)` evaluates a regular expression pattern against the target string in Chai BDD, returning true if the string satisfies the regular expression format specified. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2800,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What happens if an unhandled JavaScript error (such as a TypeError on undefined) occurs inside a `pm.test` block?","options":["The entire Postman desktop app crashes","The specific test fails and logs the error stack in Test Results, while remaining tests continue to run","Postman skips all subsequent collections permanently","The HTTP response is automatically resent"],"answer":1,"explanation":"When an exception or assertion failure occurs inside a `pm.test()` block callback, Postman catches the failure, marks that individual test as \"FAIL\" with the associated error message, and proceeds to execute any subsequent test blocks in the script."},
{"id":2801,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the purpose of `pm.response.to.be.success` in Postman test assertions?","options":["It asserts that the HTTP response code is anywhere in the 2xx range (200-299)","It asserts that the HTTP response code is strictly 200 OK","It checks that the database committed transactions without errors","It verifies that response time was under 100ms"],"answer":0,"explanation":"`pm.response.to.be.success` is a built-in Postman shorthand assertion that verifies the HTTP response status code is within the 2xx range (200 to 299 inclusive), providing a quick check for successful fulfillment."},
{"id":2802,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does `pm.response.to.be.clientError` assert in Postman?","options":["That the HTTP status code is in the 4xx range (400-499)","That the client computer lost Wi-Fi connection","That the Postman app encountered a script compilation error","That the request timed out before reaching DNS"],"answer":0,"explanation":"`pm.response.to.be.clientError` asserts that the server responded with an HTTP 4xx status code (e.g., 400, 401, 403, 404, 422), confirming that client error handling is behaving as intended. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2803,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does `pm.response.to.be.serverError` assert in Postman?","options":["That the HTTP status code is in the 5xx range (500-599)","That the database server crashed","That the client is unauthorized","That the response body is empty"],"answer":0,"explanation":"`pm.response.to.be.serverError` asserts that the HTTP response status code is in the 5xx range (500 to 599 inclusive), which is useful when testing failure scenarios, fault injection, and circuit breakers. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2804,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you check if an array returned in a JSON response contains a specific primitive value?","options":["pm.expect(jsonData.roles).to.include(\"ADMIN\");","pm.expect(jsonData.roles).to.haveItem(\"ADMIN\");","pm.expect(jsonData.roles).containsElement(\"ADMIN\");","pm.roles.hasValue(\"ADMIN\");"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In Chai assertion syntax, `pm.expect(array).to.include(item)` checks whether the specified primitive item (string, number, boolean) exists as an element in the target array representation. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2805,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"advanced","question":"How can you iterate over an array of objects in a Postman response and assert that every object has an \"id\" property of type number?","options":["jsonData.forEach(item => { pm.expect(item.id).to.be.a(\"number\"); });","pm.expect(jsonData[*].id).to.be.number();","pm.assert.all(jsonData, item => item.id == \"number\");","jsonData.map(item => pm.test(\"isNum\", item.id));"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Standard JavaScript array methods like `forEach` can be used inside a `pm.test` block to iterate over each item in the parsed JSON array and assert that `item.id` is of type \"number\" using `pm.expect(item.id).to.be.a(\"number\")`."},
{"id":2806,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"What is Newman in the Postman ecosystem?","options":["A cloud server hosting Postman documentation","A command-line collection runner for Postman that enables continuous integration and automated testing","A desktop IDE for writing Java REST Assured code","A database migration plugin for Postman"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Newman is Postman's open-source command-line collection runner built on Node.js. It allows developers to run Postman collections and environments directly from terminal environments, scripts, Docker containers, and CI/CD pipelines (such as Jenkins, GitHub Actions, and GitLab CI)."},
{"id":2807,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"Which Node Package Manager (npm) command installs Newman globally on your development machine?","options":["npm install -g newman","npm get newman --global","npm install postman-cli -g","npx setup newman"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The command `npm install -g newman` installs Newman globally on the system, making the `newman` CLI executable accessible from any directory in the command prompt or terminal. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2808,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"beginner","question":"What is the most basic CLI command to execute an exported Postman collection JSON file using Newman?","options":["newman run collection.json","newman exec collection.json","newman start collection.json","postman run collection.json"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The fundamental syntax to run a collection via Newman is `newman run <collection_file.json>`. Newman executes all requests and test assertions in order and outputs a tabular summary report to the terminal."},
{"id":2809,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Which CLI flag is used in Newman to supply an exported environment variables JSON file?","options":["-e or --environment","-v or --variables","-p or --profile","-c or --context"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The `-e` or `--environment` option allows you to specify the path or URL to a Postman environment JSON file (e.g., `newman run collection.json -e environment.json`), replacing environment variable placeholders during execution."},
{"id":2810,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Which CLI flag is used in Newman to specify a global variables file?","options":["-g or --globals","-G or --global-vars","--all-vars","-env:global"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The `-g` or `--globals` flag passes an exported global variables JSON file to the Newman run (e.g., `newman run collection.json -g globals.json`), allowing global scope placeholders to be populated during execution."},
{"id":2811,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"How do you run a Postman collection hosted on a remote URL using Newman?","options":["newman run \"https://api.postman.com/collections/<id>?apikey=<key>\"","newman fetch \"https://url\" --run","newman remote --url=\"https://url\"","newman pull-and-run \"https://url\""],"answer":0,"explanation":"Newman supports URLs directly as arguments to `newman run`. Supplying a Postman API Collection URL with an API key parameter allows Newman to fetch and run the latest collection directly from the cloud without manual file export."},
{"id":2812,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Which Newman CLI flag controls how many times (iterations) the collection should run consecutively?","options":["-n or --iteration-count","-r or --repeat","-l or --loops","-c or --count"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The `-n` or `--iteration-count <number>` parameter specifies the number of times the collection should be iterated through. It is particularly useful for load testing or cycling through rows of data files."},
{"id":2813,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Which flag in Newman specifies a delay in milliseconds between individual requests within a run?","options":["--delay-request <ms>","--sleep <ms>","--pause <ms>","--throttle <ms>"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The `--delay-request <ms>` option specifies the amount of time in milliseconds to wait between consecutive requests. This is useful for avoiding rate-limiting thresholds on target test servers. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2814,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"advanced","question":"What is the purpose of the `--bail` flag in Newman?","options":["It causes Newman to halt execution immediately upon encountering the first test failure or error","It ignores all test failures and forces a zero exit code","It suppresses terminal output to run silently","It retries failed requests up to three times"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The `--bail` flag instructs Newman to abort the entire test run immediately upon encountering the first test assertion failure or request execution error, preventing unnecessary execution of downstream dependent tests."},
{"id":2815,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"Which Newman reporter option outputs standard test results into JUnit XML format for integration with Jenkins/CI dashboards?","options":["-r cli,junit","-r xml-output","--format=junit","--report-type jenkins"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The `-r` or `--reporters` flag accepts comma-separated reporter names. Passing `-r cli,junit` produces both command-line terminal output and an XML report in JUnit format, which CI servers like Jenkins parse to display test trend charts."},
{"id":2816,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"advanced","question":"What popular Newman HTML reporter plugin generates rich, interactive, themeable HTML test reports?","options":["newman-reporter-htmlextra","newman-html-dashboard","newman-allure-reporter","postman-html-generator"],"answer":0,"explanation":"`newman-reporter-htmlextra` is an open-source reporter package for Newman that produces comprehensive, beautifully styled HTML dashboards containing request/response payloads, timing breakdowns, test scripts, and failure logs. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
{"id":2817,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What does the `--suppress-exit-code` CLI flag do in Newman?","options":["It forces Newman to exit with status code 0 (success) even if test failures occurred","It hides all error logs from stdout","It prevents OS process termination","It runs Newman in a background daemon thread"],"answer":0,"explanation":"By default, Newman exits with a non-zero exit code (e.g., 1) when any test fails. The `--suppress-exit-code` flag forces Newman to exit with code 0 regardless of failures, preventing CI pipelines from immediately aborting subsequent pipeline stages."},
{"id":2818,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"In Postman, what is the role of the \"Postman Interceptor\" extension?","options":["It acts as a proxy to capture HTTP/HTTPS traffic directly from the browser and sync browser cookies into Postman","It decompiles native iOS and Android binaries","It injects SQL injection payloads automatically into form inputs","It restarts the local operating system network interface"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Postman Interceptor is a Chrome extension and desktop companion that captures network traffic directly from the web browser and automatically syncs browser cookies and headers into the Postman desktop client session."},
{"id":2819,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What is the purpose of the \"Manage Cookies\" modal in Postman?","options":["To view, edit, add, or delete domain-specific HTTP cookies stored in Postman's native cookie jar","To clear browser caching of downloaded CSS files","To configure session timeouts for Postman cloud accounts","To bake cryptographic tokens for JWT validation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The \"Cookies\" modal (Manage Cookies) allows users to inspect, modify, create, or delete session and persistent cookies stored across specific domains in Postman's programmatic cookie jar, replicating real browser session persistence."},
{"id":2820,"topic":"api-postman","difficulty":"intermediate","question":"What happens when you use `postman.setNextRequest(\"Request Name\")` inside a Postman test script?","options":["It immediately executes the named request synchronously within the current test script","It defines the next request to be executed during a Collection Runner or Newman execution workflow","It opens a new tab in the Postman desktop user interface","It deletes the preceding request from the collection history"],"answer":1,"explanation":"`postman.setNextRequest(\"Request Name\")` changes the default sequential execution order during automated Collection Runner or Newman runs, allowing conditional branching, loops, and custom test workflow orchestration across collection executions. This foundational concept is essential in modern API test automation and quality engineering workflows to ensure high reliability, comprehensive coverage, and robust validation across distributed microservices."},
]);
